.TH icheck "" "Administration"
.SH NAME
icheck \- i-node consistency check
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBicheck [\-sv] [\-b \fIblock ...\fB] \fIfilesystem ...\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.HS
icheck -- i-node consistency check
Usage:	icheck [ options ] filesystem ...
Options:
	-b	Any following numeric arguments are taken to be block numbers;
		all references to these blocks are printed, with the type
	-s	Make some repairs on the file system (requires write access)
	-v	Print a summary of information about the file system
.HE
.B icheck
reads the super-block and i-list of each
.I filesystem
and builds a map of every block in use, checking as it goes that no
block is claimed by more than one i-node, by both an i-node and the
free list, or more than once within the free list or within a
single i-node's block list.
.PP
Each
.I filesystem
should normally be unmounted, and read access to its special file is
enough unless
.B \-s
is given.
.SH Options
.IP \fB\-b\fR 0.4i
Every argument after this option, up to the next option or the end
of the argument list, is a block number rather than a
.IR filesystem ;
as the scan proceeds,
.B icheck
prints, with its type, every reference it finds to any of these
blocks.
.IP \fB\-s\fR
Ignore the existing free list, note every block claimed by an
i-node, and rebuild the super-block's free list and free i-node
list from what remains; this requires write access to
.IR filesystem .
.IP \fB\-v\fR
Print a summary of file, block, and free-space counts for
.I filesystem
after checking it.
.PP
.B icheck
runs on each named
.I filesystem
in turn and exits with the bitwise OR of the exit status from
every one:
.DS
.ta 8n
\fB0x01\fR	miscellaneous error (e.g., cannot open, out of memory)
\fB0x02\fR	an i/o error made the check impossible to complete
\fB0x04\fR	bad free list
\fB0x08\fR	missing blocks (in neither an i-node nor the free list)
\fB0x10\fR	duplicate blocks in the free list
\fB0x20\fR	the free-i-node count in the super-block is wrong
.DE
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR check ,
.BR dcheck ,
.BR mount ,
.B sync
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.B icheck
prints a line for each bad or duplicate block it finds, giving the
block number, the type of reference (bootstrap, super block, inodes,
direct, indirect, double indirect, triple indirect, dir, bad, or
free), and, for a duplicate, the i-number involved.
It also reports
.B "Ridiculous fsize/isize"
and gives up on a
.I filesystem
whose super-block looks wrong, and
.B "Bad ifree list"
if the free-i-node count does not match what it counted.
.SH NOTES
Filesystem sizes and structures newer than this port's fixed
addressing limits (10 direct, 1 single-, 1 double-, and 1
triple-indirect block per i-node) are not supported.
